60,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"Um... looking at these, I think Candidate C, the Supplier Data Hub, is the best. It directly addresses the misrouted deliveries and the time wasted copying records that I found. It solves the actual problems on the floor.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, I love that it solves the problem, but look at the ""shared update and review procedures."" Who is actually doing that updating? If it's the coordination group, they'll collapse. We need to be very careful here.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, for once, your pessimism is useful. Candidate A is a non-starter; six-week device approvals and dead zones make a ""mobile app"" a joke. Candidate C is the only one that's actually rational, provided it's a fixed-fee build.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, I agree that A is too risky. However, Candidate C is a broad, cross-unit tool. My research shows these usually fail. We must ensure it fits the software allowance and doesn't require capital for new servers.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Ryuji, you're right! Candidate C looks like those big standardization efforts that usually end up without an owner. If it requires everyone to update records daily, it'll just disappear like the others. Maybe Candidate B or D is safer?","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Ryuji, @Kenji... I understand your worries, but the misrouted deliveries are happening because of different addresses. If we don't fix that, the waste continues. Could we make Candidate C very narrow, just for addresses, to make it survive?","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, narrowing it helps, but ""shared procedures"" still sounds like a trap for the staff. What about Candidate D? It automates summaries and notes—that actually takes work *off* the plate of people like the coordination group!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, Candidate D is a toy. It's a convenience, not a solution to a structural failure. If we're being rational, Candidate C is the only one addressing a real cost, provided we avoid per-head licensing.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, while C addresses a cost, it risks the ""broad tool"" failure pattern. However, D is a general utility that might not fit a specific budget line. We must prioritize a project that is financially sustainable and narrow.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Ryuji, @Shunichi, if we want a ""small win,"" maybe Candidate B is the way? It's a specific tool for skill transfer, not a broad system everyone must use daily. It feels more like the tool room success!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@Kenji, @Ryuji... I'm sorry, but Candidate B doesn't help the people struggling with the delivery errors or the copying. If we pick something just because it's ""safe,"" we're ignoring the real pain on the floor. Please, can we consider C?","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, I hear you, but we can't ignore the staffing void! Candidate C's ""shared procedures"" are a recipe for burnout. Candidate D actually reduces the manual burden on our exhausted staff. Isn't that the real win?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, reducing a few meeting notes is a trivial gain compared to the systemic waste Daisuke identified. Candidate C is the only one with a real ROI, provided we implement it as a fixed-fee project.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May"",""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, ROI is irrelevant if the funding source is wrong. Candidate C's cross-unit nature suggests a capital expense for infrastructure, which is frozen. Candidate D is a software-based utility that likely fits the recurring allowance.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Ryuji, that's a great point! If C is too big and expensive, it'll just fail like the other standardization projects. D is small and helpful, but B is also very focused. Which one is more sustainable?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I really believe we should choose Candidate C. Even if it's risky, the daily waste is too much to ignore. Please, let's help the people who are struggling with these errors every single day.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, I want to help them, but not by giving them a new burden! Candidate D is the only one that actually gives time back to the staff. Let's pick D and stop the burnout!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, your obsession with ""burnout"" is blinding you to the fact that Candidate D is a luxury, not a necessity. Candidate C solves a structural failure. If we are rational, we pick C and manage the scope.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, managing scope does not change the budget source. Candidate C's cross-unit nature is a red flag for both financial and operational failure. Candidate D is the only sustainable, low-risk option that fits our current constraints. I vote for D.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"@Ryuji, you've convinced me! Candidate C is too risky and fits the pattern of projects that just disappear. Candidate D is a small, sustainable win that won't burn anyone out. I vote for D!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
